    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 5 (Questions 25&ndash;30)</div>\n<p>For more than a century, nineteenth- and early-twentieth-century Eurocentric historiography was dominated by a rigid, positivist epistemological maxim: &ldquo;no written documents, no history.&rdquo; Societies without alphabetic writing systems&mdash;particularly across sub-Saharan Africa, the indigenous Americas, and Oceania&mdash;were summarily relegated to the realm of prehistory, characterized as static, ahistorical cultures trapped in an unchanging mythological consciousness. Belgian historian Jan Vansina executed a decisive intellectual revolution against this deeply entrenched prejudice with his foundational 1961 work <i>Oral Tradition: A Study in Historical Methodology</i> and its 1985 revision <i>Oral Tradition as History</i>. Conducting pioneering field research in the Kuba, Rwanda, and Burundi kingdoms of Central Africa, Vansina established that oral traditions&mdash;defined as verbal testimonies and reported statements concerning the past that have been transmitted across generations&mdash;constitute legitimate, structured historical sources capable of generating rigorous historical chronologies.</p>\n<p>Vansina demonstrated that the transmission of oral history is not a chaotic, haphazard game of telephone; rather, it is a formal social institution governed by strict institutional controls and communal surveillance. In centralized kingdoms, the preservation of historical knowledge was entrusted to professional hereditary bards, court genealogists, and ritual priests who underwent decades of demanding formal training. These custodians utilized sophisticated mnemonic devices, including fixed poetic meters, royal praise poetry, proverbs, and prescribed musical cadences, which established rigid linguistic constraints that prevented verbatim drift over centuries. Furthermore, recitation occurred in highly formalized public spaces where elderly dignitaries and peers acted as vigilant adjudicators, ready to correct any deviation or unauthorized embellishment. Vansina established a typology of oral genres&mdash;such as official king lists, dynastic panegyrics, legal precedents, and etiologic tales&mdash;demonstrating that different genres possessed varying degrees of structural stability, cultural intent, and susceptibility to conscious ideological manipulation.</p>\n<p>Crucially, Vansina did not advocate an uncritical, romantic acceptance of oral lore. Instead, he formulated a rigorous hermeneutic methodology designed to decipher the systematic distortions inherent in oral transmission. Foremost among these dynamics is &ldquo;structural amnesia,&rdquo; an anthropological phenomenon whereby oral genealogies are continuously modified to conform to contemporary political realities. In lineage-based societies, ancestral links and branches that no longer validate present territorial boundaries, chieftaincy succession claims, or tribute obligations are quietly excised from communal memory, while usurping dynasts routinely graft their own ancestors onto ancient royal lineages to manufacture political legitimacy. Closely intertwined with this is the &ldquo;hourglass effect&rdquo; (or floating gap) of oral chronologies. Informants typically possess voluminous, richly detailed mythological accounts concerning the primordial origins of the world and the founder of the dynasty, alongside highly detailed memories of the most recent four or five generations. Between these two vivid poles lies a shadowy, compressed middle epoch, wherein centuries of complex dynastic history are telescoped into a single generation or attributed to a single legendary monarch.</p>\n<p>Structuralist anthropologists and functionalist skeptics, notably T.O. Beidelman and David Henige, mounted vigorous challenges to Vansina&rsquo;s paradigm. They argued that oral traditions function primarily as contemporary social charters designed to legitimize current power configurations, meaning they are perpetually reinvented in the present, rendering the recovery of authentic antiquity an impossible fantasy. Vansina countered by demonstrating that oral traditions cannot be dismissed as pure fiction. When subjected to rigorous internal source criticism and cross-referenced through external triangulation&mdash;corroborating oral king lists against astronomical records of solar eclipses, archaeological excavations, and comparative historical linguistics&mdash;oral testimonies yield robust, verifiable historical chronologies. In demonstrating this, Vansina dismantled the colonial myth of the &ldquo;people without history,&rdquo; establishing oral tradition as a vital, rigorous pillar of global historical consciousness.</p>",
