    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 7 (Questions 37&ndash;42)</div>\n<p>In his monumental 1963 classic <i>The Making of the English Working Class</i> and his seminal 1967 essay &ldquo;Time, Work-Discipline, and Industrial Capitalism,&rdquo; historian E.P. Thompson executed an intellectual revolution in modern social history. Thompson mounted a sustained, devastating assault against both orthodox economic reductionism and crude Marxist determinism. For generations, economic historians had treated the working class as an inert, passive byproduct of mechanical invention&mdash;a demographic mass automatically generated by the steam engine, the spinning jenny, and the mechanized cotton mill. Thompson inverted this mechanistic teleology, famously declaring in his preface that &ldquo;the working class did not rise like the sun at an appointed time. It was present at its own making.&rdquo; Class, Thompson insisted, is not an abstract static structure, a quantitative sociological bracket, or a mere economic category; rather, it is an active cultural relationship and an unfolding historical phenomenon, forged through shared experiences, traditions, moral values, and collective struggles across time.</p>\n<p>A foundational pillar of Thompson&rsquo;s analysis was the profound psychic and cultural trauma wrought by the restructuring of temporal perception under industrial capitalism. In pre-industrial agrarian and artisan economies, labor was organized around what Thompson called &ldquo;task-orientation.&rdquo; Time was experienced organically, governed by natural diurnal cycles, changing seasons, agricultural necessities, and the specific demands of the craft. Artisans and agricultural laborers alternated bouts of intense, concentrated labor with extended periods of communal leisure, holy days, and customary festivities such as &ldquo;Saint Monday&rdquo;&mdash;the widespread artisanal tradition of taking Mondays off to recover from weekend revelry and assert craft autonomy. With the advent of centralized factory production and costly mechanized capital equipment, task-oriented time was ruthlessly supplanted by synchronized &ldquo;clock-time.&rdquo; Industrial employers recognized that an idle machine represented uncaptured surplus value. Consequently, time became a rigid, commodified metric: time was no longer passed, but spent, saved, bought, and measured in strict monetary increments.</p>\n<p>This imposition of factory work-discipline provoked fierce, prolonged plebeian resistance. Workers did not experience the factory horn and clock as neutral markers of technical efficiency, but as despotic instruments of social alienation. Thompson documented how the emerging proletariat mobilized traditional cultural defenses against the ruthless logic of laissez-faire political economy. Foremost among these defenses was the &ldquo;moral economy of the crowd.&rdquo; In eighteenth-century food riots, rural laborers and urban artisans did not act as an irrational, starving mob driven by animal desperation. Rather, they mobilized around a coherent, shared consensus: a belief that traditional community welfare, paternalistic obligation, and customary &ldquo;just prices&rdquo; took absolute precedence over free-market profiteering, hoarding, and speculative price-gouging. When grain merchants artificially inflated prices during shortages, crowds seized grain shipments, sold them at traditional fair prices, and peacefully remitted the proceeds to the merchants, asserting a moral imperative against unfettered capitalist market forces.</p>\n<p>Subsequent social historians and feminist scholars have qualified aspects of Thompson&rsquo;s vision. Critics point out that Thompson tended to romanticize pre-industrial artisanal life, downplaying the grinding poverty, patriarchal domestic domination, and child exploitation endemic to traditional cottage industries. Furthermore, post-colonial critics observe that Thompson analyzed the English working class in relative isolation from the broader British imperial apparatus, overlooking how domestic industrialization and working-class living standards were structurally subsidized by colonial plantation slavery and imperial resource extraction. Nevertheless, Thompson&rsquo;s contribution permanently transformed modern historical scholarship. By demonstrating that ordinary plebeians were conscious historical agents who actively contested the commodification of their time and labor, Thompson proved that the triumph of industrial capitalism was never an inevitable technological destiny, but a bitterly fought political and cultural struggle.</p>",
