    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 8 (Questions 43&ndash;48)</div>\n<p>For much of the twentieth century, the historiography of the French Revolution of 1789 was anchored by the &ldquo;classical&rdquo; Marxist social interpretation, championed by preeminent French historians Georges Lefebvre and Albert Soboul. Rooted in the principles of historical materialism, this orthodoxy conceptualized the Revolution as an objective, necessary culmination of socioeconomic class conflict. In Lefebvre&rsquo;s influential formulation, the Revolution represented the historic triumph of an ascending, commercially vibrant bourgeoisie over an entrenched, parasitic feudal aristocracy. The abolition of seigneurial dues, aristocratic tax immunities, and corporatist trade guilds was interpreted as a structural prerequisite that swept away archaic feudal fetters, clearing the historical terrain for the consolidation of modern industrial capitalism. In this narrative, the political upheaval was directly determined by underlying economic transformations.</p>\n<p>Beginning in the 1960s, however, this Marxist consensus encountered a devastating empirical and conceptual challenge from Anglo-American and French revisionist historians. Archival investigations by Alfred Cobban, George V. Taylor, and Colin Lucas exposed profound empirical contradictions in the Marxist model. Revisionists demonstrated that the late-eighteenth-century French bourgeoisie was not an aggressive vanguard of industrial capitalists; rather, it was an overwhelmingly conservative social stratum composed of venal office-holders, legal professionals, and rentiers whose wealth was proprietary and land-based, virtually indistinguishable from that of the liberal nobility. Furthermore, nobles and bourgeois were socially and culturally integrated, sharing identical economic portfolios and collaborating harmoniously within Enlightenment salons and provincial academies. The Revolution could not have been a clash between two antagonistic economic modes of production, revisionists argued, because no distinct industrial bourgeoisie existed to wage such a class war.</p>\n<p>The definitive intellectual blow against the Marxist paradigm was delivered by French historian Fran&ccedil;ois Furet in his incendiary 1978 work, <i>Penser la R&eacute;volution fran&ccedil;aise</i> (<i>Interpreting the French Revolution</i>). Furet radically shifted the historiographical axis from socioeconomic structures to the autonomy of political culture and revolutionary discourse. Furet famously proclaimed that &ldquo;the French Revolution is over,&rdquo; calling upon scholars to abandon partisan passions and analyze the Revolution as a self-contained ideological phenomenon. For Furet, the collapse of absolute monarchy in 1789 generated a massive power vacuum that was occupied not by competing social classes, but by competing representations of the &ldquo;General Will.&rdquo; Crucially, Furet inverted the Marxist interpretation of the Reign of Terror (1793&ndash;1794). While Lefebvre and Soboul excused the Terror as an accidental <i>d&eacute;rapage</i> (skid)&mdash;a temporary emergency measure forced upon the Jacobins by foreign war and counterrevolutionary plots&mdash;Furet insisted that the Terror was the inherent, logical outcome of revolutionary ideology itself. By embracing Rousseauian notions of an indivisible popular sovereignty, revolutionary language branded any political disagreement as treason against the nation, creating a totalitarian dynamic wherein radical political discourse ruled through paranoia and the guillotine.</p>\n<p>Contemporary post-revisionist historians, including Lynn Hunt and Colin Jones, have sought to move beyond the polarized binary between Lefebvre&rsquo;s socioeconomic determinism and Furet&rsquo;s linguistic idealism. Post-revisionists argue that while Furet brilliantly illuminated the totalitarian mechanisms of Jacobin political rhetoric, his radical focus on discourse risked reducing the Revolution to a disembodied linguistic event, ignoring the palpable material anxieties, subsistence crises, and rural peasant rebellions that fueled revolutionary mobilization. Today, the French Revolution is increasingly conceptualized not as an inevitable bourgeois victory nor as a pure linguistic pathology, but as an open-ended, volatile dialectic between structural material pressures and the dynamic invention of modern democratic politics.</p>",
