    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 9 (Questions 49&ndash;53)</div>\n<p>In their landmark 1985 study <i>Leviathan and the Air-Pump: Hobbes, Boyle, and the Experimental Life</i>, sociologists of science Steven Shapin and Simon Schaffer mounted a decisive assault on the foundational mythology of modern science. For centuries, orthodox histories of the Scientific Revolution celebrated Robert Boyle&rsquo;s mid-seventeenth-century air-pump experiments as the definitive triumph of empirical observation over scholastic metaphysics. In this triumphalist narrative, Boyle&rsquo;s experimental method permitted nature to &ldquo;speak for itself,&rdquo; establishing objective &ldquo;matters of fact&rdquo; free from the distorting influences of politics, theology, and human prejudice. Shapin and Schaffer dismantled this canonical hagiography. Applying the sociology of scientific knowledge, they demonstrated that scientific facts are not transparent reflections of the physical world waiting to be passively discovered; rather, they are socially constructed artifacts produced through specific cultural conventions, literary practices, and institutional mechanisms of credibility.</p>\n<p>Shapin and Schaffer illuminated these mechanics by recovering the forgotten, intense controversy between Robert Boyle and political philosopher Thomas Hobbes. Hobbes, author of <i>Leviathan</i>, launched a ferocious epistemological assault on Boyle&rsquo;s experimental program. Hobbes argued that the artificial vacuum produced inside Boyle&rsquo;s pneumatic air-pump was an artifact of a leaky, fragile apparatus rather than a pristine revelation of nature. More fundamentally, Hobbes attacked the political vulnerability of the experimental method. Having lived through the catastrophic violence of the English Civil War, Hobbes maintained that political order required an absolute, indivisible sovereign authority whose truths could not be contested. He argued that private experimental associations like the Royal Society, where small coteries of philosophers debated ambiguous experimental results behind closed laboratory doors, would generate factionalism, dispute, and civil discord. For Hobbes, genuine philosophy had to be deductive, demonstrative, and universally binding&mdash;modeled on geometry&mdash;rather than probabilistic and dependent on fallible machines.</p>\n<p>To establish the authority of experimental knowledge against Hobbesian skepticism, Boyle engineered three interlocking technologies: material, literary, and social. The material technology was the pneumatic engine itself, an intricate and expensive machine that served as a tangible theater of empirical demonstration. The literary technology consisted of Boyle&rsquo;s meticulous, unadorned prose style. By publishing exhaustive, plain-style accounts detailing experimental setups, failed trials, and circumstantial laboratory conditions, Boyle enabled &ldquo;virtual witnessing&rdquo; for distant readers who could not physically attend the trials. Finally, Boyle&rsquo;s social technology defined the criteria for credible witnesses. Boyle restricted witnessing to the gentlemanly culture of Restoration England. In early modern society, only wealthy gentlemen were considered morally reliable and disinterested observers, because their independent economic wealth purportedly rendered them immune to bribery, professional bias, or political coercion. Scientific truth was thus explicitly anchored in the class privilege and honor codes of the English gentry.</p>\n<p>Scientific realists and philosophical rationalists have strongly contested Shapin and Schaffer&rsquo;s conclusions, arguing that their historical sociology borders on a self-defeating epistemological relativism. Realists point out that regardless of seventeenth-century gentlemanly social codes, Boyle&rsquo;s air-pump tapped into genuine physical realities governed by universal natural laws: a candle flame is extinguished in a vacuum whether witnessed by an English aristocrat or a peasant laborer. Yet Shapin and Schaffer never denied the existence of physical reality; rather, they demonstrated that the social acceptance of what counts as verified knowledge is fundamentally political. By revealing how Boyle&rsquo;s experimental community was co-produced with the political settlement of the English Restoration, Shapin and Schaffer substantiated their enduring maxim: &ldquo;solutions to the problem of knowledge are solutions to the problem of social order.&rdquo;</p>",
